  <a href="#section-how-to-use" id="section-how-to-use" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>How to use</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Say a nightly import finishes and you want its job to send a short status email.
      <a href="download.html#navigation">Download</a> and unpack the standalone CLI, then use the <code>sjm</code> launcher for Windows or Unix.
      With a relay <a href="#section-config-properties">configured in properties</a>, the job can send directly.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-text">sjm send --email:startingBlank --email:from jobs@example.org --email:to ops@example.org --email:withSubject "Nightly import" --email:withPlainText "The import finished."</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide" id="packaged-cli-metadata">
      The distribution includes the command and help data prepared during its build.
      Normal commands only load that data; they never regenerate it or write replacements. If the CLI reports missing or incompatible metadata,
      reinstall the complete distribution. If you are working from source, use the <a href="#building-cli-from-source">normal Maven build</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      OAuth2 over implicit TLS uses the existing <code>--mailer:withTransportStrategy SMTPS_OAUTH2</code> option.
      Supply the access token as the SMTP password through the existing server option or loaded configuration; the default port is 465.
      Runtime token-provider objects are Java integrations. See <a href="security.html#section-transport-strategy-smtps-oauth2">the security guide</a>
      for the fixed-token and provider forms.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">Each builder flag calls its Java method in command-line order.</p>
    <details class="doc-disclosure wide">
      <summary>What happens when you repeat a flag</summary>
      <p><code>--email:to alice@example.org --email:to bob@example.org</code> adds both recipients. A display name
        applies only to its occurrence. For example, <code>--email:to "Alice" alice@example.org --email:to bob@example.org</code>
        names Alice and leaves Bob unnamed.</p>
      <p>Attachment and header additions accumulate. Replacement and clear/reset flags take effect where they occur.
        <code>--email:withSubject "Draft" --email:withSubject "Final"</code> leaves the subject as <code>Final</code>.</p>
    </details>
    <p class="wide" id="integer-option-values">
      Integer options accept whole decimal numbers within the signed 32-bit range. Boolean words, fractions and overflowing values produce an
      argument error. For example, use <code>--mailer:withSessionTimeout 10000</code> for ten seconds, or <code>0</code> for unlimited socket
      timeouts. <code>false</code> is invalid for this numeric option. Each option's own range and units still apply; Boolean options accept
      their usual Boolean values. See the <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#cli-integer-values">integer-input migration guidance</a> when upgrading.
    </p>
    <h4>Start from a message file</h4>
    <p class="wide">Suppose you saved a nightly report as EML and want to check it with a clearer subject.
      Copy it into a new draft and rehearse that draft without opening an SMTP connection.</p>
<pre class="wide"><code class="language-shell">sjm validate --email:copying nightly-report.eml --email:withSubject "Nightly import results"</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">For a finished invoice that needs to go out as saved, use
      <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-exact-eml">exact EML</a> and set the envelope recipient explicitly.</p>
<pre class="wide"><code class="language-shell">sjm send --email:startingFromExactEml invoice-2048.eml --email:withEnvelopeRecipients ap@example.net</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">For EML or Outlook MSG, <code>--email:copying</code> edits a draft,
      <code>--email:replyingTo</code> starts a reply, and <code>--email:forwarding</code> starts a forward.
      The CLI identifies the format from the contents.</p>
    <details class="doc-disclosure wide" id="message-file-import-limits">
      <summary>Import limits for copies, replies and forwards</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Copy or reply.</strong> These options import through <code>MimeMessage</code> and use the request factory's
          <a href="configuration.html#section-import-limits">part and depth limits</a>. A finite
          <code>simplejavamail.conversion.import.maximuminputbytes</code> fails before reading the file.</li>
        <li><strong>Forward.</strong> EML forwarding borrows the MIME message. MSG forwarding imports it and applies its supported limits.</li>
        <li><strong>Cap the original file size.</strong> Use raw input through the
          <a href="/email-conversion.html#conversion-import-limits">Java converter</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <h4>Add recipients and require TLS for the whole route</h4>
    <p class="wide">If the report contains confidential status information, you might copy the data team, BCC the audit mailbox,
      and require TLS for onward delivery. Here's the command in a Unix shell. It reads the relay credentials from shell variables.</p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-bash">sjm send \
  --email:startingBlank \
  --email:from "Import jobs" jobs@example.org \
  --email:to "Operations" ops@example.org \
  --email:cc data-team@example.org \
  --email:bcc audit@example.org \
  --email:withSubject "Nightly import" \
  --email:withPlainText "The nightly import finished. See the jobs dashboard for details." \
  --email:withTlsRequiredForOnwardDelivery \
  --mailer:withSMTPServer smtp.example.org 587 "$SMTP_USER" "$SMTP_PASSWORD" \
  --mailer:withTransportStrategy SMTP_TLS</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      The REQUIRETLS flag is per Email and asks supporting relays to keep TLS mandatory after the first server accepts the message; it is separate from
      the Mailer's first-hop transport strategy. Exact EML uses <code class="inline language-text">--email:withExactTlsRequiredForOnwardDelivery</code>.
      A send fails before <code class="inline language-text">MAIL FROM</code> when the actual connection cannot honor the request. See
      <a href="security.html#section-requiretls">Require TLS for onward delivery</a> for the trust, capability, and reporting contract.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also check the <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/tree/develop/modules/cli-module/src/test/java/demo">demo code</a>
      included in the sources.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section>
    <h4>Rehearse a send without contacting the relay</h4>
    <p class="wide">Before you put the job on a schedule, run the same command with <code>validate</code> in place of <code>send</code>.</p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code class="inline language-text">sjm validate</code> applies the selected Mailer defaults and overrides and rehearses the complete message,
      including MIME construction, configured S/MIME, OpenPGP and DKIM processing, and the final encoded-size limit. It starts no SMTP connections.
      Use Mailer options with the command when those settings are part of what you want to validate.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      One-shot validation and daemon cache misses prepare transiently without starting sending workers or connection pools, even when a
      nonzero pool core size is configured. A daemon can reuse an already ready, compatible Mailer; its existing pools continue independently.
      Validation does not populate the sending cache or wait for another command's Mailer startup. A later <code>send</code> still activates
      its normal configured pool. The bundled batch module remains available throughout.
    </p>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-cli-recipient-rejection" id="section-cli-recipient-rejection" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Choose whether a recipient rejection stops sending</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>Add <code>--email:withSendingToAcceptedRecipients true</code> to continue for accepted recipients when another address is rejected,
        or use <code>false</code> to withhold content after a known rejection. Exact EML uses
        <code>--email:withExactSendingToAcceptedRecipients true</code> (or <code>false</code>); its bytes remain unchanged.</p>
      <p>Omit the option to retain defaults and advanced-provider behavior. Fresh request builders start unset, so there is no CLI clear option.
        The ordinary and locked properties also work through the CLI's configuration-file loading. Partial acceptance still has a failed exit status;
        these options do not split messages or retry them. See
        <a href="analyzing-send-results.html#section-partial-send">both choices, receipts and the advanced sendpartial conflict</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-legacy-smtp-content" id="section-legacy-smtp-content" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Use a verified legacy SMTP route</h3>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">The CLI uses the same per-message negotiation as Java. If you have verified that your legacy server and its onward route preserve
      UTF-8 or 8-bit data without advertising support, enable this option:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">sjm send --email:startingBlank --email:from sender@example.org --email:to müller@example.org --email:withPlainText Hello --mailer:withSMTPServer legacy-relay.example.org 587 --mailer:withLegacySmtpContentSupport true</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">The equivalent loaded property is <code>simplejavamail.smtp.legacycontentsupport=true</code>; the default is false.
      The option participates in the daemon's Mailer configuration profile, so requests with different choices do not share a Mailer.
      Use <code>--mailer:withLegacySmtpContentSupport--help</code> for its full contract. TLS and content-safety checks remain enforced;
      successful submission is not proof of onward delivery. See the <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-international-mail">international-mail guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-probe" id="section-probe" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Inspect an SMTP connection</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Use <code>sjm connect</code> for a simple connection check, or <code>sjm probe</code> to see what the server advertised and what happened
      during TLS setup. A probe opens a fresh connection, prints the same report as <code>mailer.sync().probeConnection()</code>, and closes
      that connection without sending an email. It connects even when logging-only mode is enabled.
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-text">sjm probe --mailer:withSMTPServer smtp.example.org 587 --mailer:withTransportStrategy SMTP_TLS

# With SMTP settings and credentials already supplied through configuration:
sjm probe --authenticate

# Use the daemon's configuration and return the report to this terminal:
sjm probe --daemon=require --authenticate</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      SMTP authentication is off by default, even when credentials are configured. <code>--authenticate</code> opts in for this invocation
      only; it does not change later probes or sends. Configured proxy authentication and TLS client certificates still apply without that flag.
      Authentication follows your TLS settings, so check the endpoint and policy before opting in. Keep passwords out of shell history;
      use the existing <a href="#section-config-properties">configuration sources</a> instead.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The authentication flag controls the dedicated probe connection, not existing send pools. If you configured a nonzero connection-pool
      core size, building the Mailer can also open and authenticate pooled send connections. For a one-shot diagnostic without that warm-up,
      use <code>--mailer:withConnectionPoolCoreSize 0</code>, which is already the default, and check any per-cluster core-size overrides.
      This does not stop pools already running in a daemon.
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-text">SMTP connection probe: SUCCESS
Endpoint: smtp.example.org:587 (smtp)
Greeting: 220 smtp.example.org ready
Before TLS: {SIZE=[10485760], STARTTLS=[]}
STARTTLS: completed
After TLS: {AUTH=[LOGIN PLAIN], DSN=[], SIZE=[26214400]}
...
Authentication: succeeded (LOGIN)
Elapsed: 142ms</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      This sample is abbreviated. The <a href="debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">SMTP diagnostics guide</a> explains the full report,
      including partial failures, unavailable metadata, and the separate capability lists before and after STARTTLS.
      Advertised support does not prove a later email will be accepted or delivered.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The report goes to stdout, including failed and unsupported reports. Exit code <code>0</code> means the probe succeeded;
      <code>3</code> means it failed or is unsupported. Invalid arguments return <code>2</code>. Ordinary execution errors are written to stderr;
      daemon routing retains its existing exit codes. A daemon may reuse the Mailer, but each probe still opens and closes its own SMTP connection.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Run <code>sjm probe --help</code> for Mailer options or <code>sjm probe --authenticate--help</code> for the authentication flag.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-daemon" id="section-daemon" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Optional background daemon</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">If the nightly import sends a status email for each account, start the per-user daemon before the job.
      Check the relay through it and confirm it's running.</p>
<pre class="wide"><code class="language-shell">sjm daemon start
sjm connect -d
sjm daemon status</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">One-shot commands exit by default. Add <code>-d</code>, <code>--daemon</code> or <code>--daemon=acquire</code>
      to use a running daemon or start one. Add <code>--daemon=require</code> to require a running instance or
      <code>--daemon=off</code> to force one-shot execution. These choices apply to <code>send</code>, <code>connect</code>,
      <code>probe</code> and <code>validate</code>. Set <code>--daemon-instance=work</code> when an instance needs its own
      configuration or lifecycle.</p>
    <details class="doc-disclosure wide">
      <summary>Where the daemon runs and how to install it</summary>
      <p>The daemon runs as your operating-system user and authenticates each bounded request. It uses a Unix-domain socket
        or falls back to <code>127.0.0.1</code>. Use <code>-d</code> on any supported platform. Windows needs no Scheduled Task
        or Service. The portable archive includes an optional tested systemd user unit. Homebrew and Chocolatey distribution is
        tracked in <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/issues/708">issue #708</a>. See the
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/develop/modules/cli-module/DAEMON.md">daemon operations guide</a>
        for configuration, pooling, shutdown, exit codes and package-manager status.</p>
    </details>

    <h4>Reload the daemon's startup settings</h4>
    <p class="wide" id="daemon-startup-configuration">Say the relay moves to port 2525 and you edit
      <code>etc/simplejavamail.properties</code>. A command with <code>--daemon=acquire</code> still connects using
      the startup settings of the running daemon. Restart it to pick up the edit.</p>
<pre class="wide"><code class="language-shell">sjm daemon restart
sjm connect -d</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">Run <code>restart</code> with the complete startup configuration you want, including any environment
      variables or JVM settings you supplied on the last start and still need. It loads the current sources.
      Later client JVM properties leave a running daemon's defaults as they were. Explicit Mailer flags on a request
      still follow the normal conflict policy and locks.</p>
    <details class="doc-disclosure wide">
      <summary>Named failover and startup property precedence</summary>
      <p>Detached startup preserves all supported Simple Java Mail JVM settings, including
        <code>simplejavamail.mailers.*</code> definitions and their <code>simplejavamail.locked.mailers.*</code> settings.
        A primary's named failover keeps its complete definition. Files, environment variables, source precedence and locks
        retain their normal rules. Blank JVM values leave environment values in place. Supported typed JVM values take
        precedence over inherited String defaults. See the
        <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#cli-daemon-startup-configuration">daemon startup configuration notes</a>.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="doc-disclosure wide" id="daemon-state-paths">
      <summary>Troubleshoot state paths and startup logs</summary>
      <p>Set <code>SJM_DAEMON_STATE_DIR</code> to choose a different, privately checked state directory. A relative path
        resolves from the invoking directory. Use the same resulting directory whenever you address the instance. An absolute
        path helps when your commands run from different working directories.</p>
<pre><code class="language-shell">export SJM_DAEMON_STATE_DIR=./sjm-state
sjm daemon start
sjm daemon status
sjm daemon stop</code></pre>
      <p>The client and detached child use the same normalized absolute state root. If a long path needs a shorter Unix-socket
        location, the commands must also resolve to the same temporary directory. See the
        <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#cli-daemon-state-paths">daemon state-path guidance</a>.</p>
      <p id="daemon-startup-log"><code>instance.lock</code>, <code>daemon.log</code> and <code>daemon-startup.log</code>
        must be regular files. Startup refuses other file types and symlinks, including dangling ones, before changing content or
        permissions. An existing directory keeps its permissions and contents, and a dangling link leaves its target absent.
        Lock acquisition also refuses symlinks. Regular state files are made private, and only the startup log is cleared
        before the next child starts.</p>
    </details>

    <h4>Know when a Mailer is reused</h4>
    <p class="wide">Requests with the same effective Mailer settings can share a retained Mailer. A different host,
      credential, proxy, trust setting or pool configuration selects a different entry. Compatible commands can share pooled
      SMTP connections with the standalone distribution's <code>batch-module</code>. A custom classpath without that module
      supports the daemon without retaining pooled transports.</p>

    <h5 id="selected-route-key-files">Rotate the signing key at the same path</h5>
    <p class="wide">Say DKIM signing is already configured and reads this key.</p>
<pre class="wide"><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.dkim.signing.private_key_file_or_data=file:/srv/mail/signing.der</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">When you replace the file, the next command sees the new key bytes and creates a Mailer for them
      instead of reusing one with the old key. Key contents on the selected route and its named failover chain affect reuse.
      One-shot and daemon commands skip unused destinations' key files. Selected-file failures, locks and explicit route
      choices still apply. Use <code>base64:</code> to supply inline key data.</p>

    <details class="doc-disclosure wide" id="daemon-mailer-capacity">
      <summary>Troubleshoot a busy response, unused keys or image paths</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A full cache.</strong> A new configuration can replace the oldest fully started, idle Mailer.
          If every slot is in use or starting, the new configuration gets a busy response. Compatible commands can still
          use their retained Mailer.</li>
        <li id="disabled-default-key-files"><strong>Unused signing files.</strong> Select <code>--mailer:withoutEmailDefaults</code>
          to skip unused DKIM and S/MIME default files, including in the reuse check. Active and locked key material still
          needs its files. <code>--mailer:clearEmailDefaults</code> restores the captured defaults. The last choice wins,
          and named Mailers keep their own defaults.</li>
        <li id="daemon-image-paths"><strong>Image files.</strong> Relative paths passed to <code>--email:withEmbeddedImageBaseDir</code>
          and relative image sources without a configured base use the client's working directory. Bases from configuration
          keep the configuration directory. Absolute paths, containment rules and URL/classpath lookup keep their ordinary behavior.</li>
      </ul>
<pre><code class="language-shell">sjm connect --mailer:withoutEmailDefaults</code></pre>
    </details>

    <h4 id="daemon-shutdown">Check progress and stop cleanly</h4>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>If your nightly import is still sending, run <code>status</code> to check daemon activity before you stop it.</p>
<pre><code class="language-shell">sjm daemon status
sjm daemon stop</code></pre>
    </div>
    <ol class="wide">
      <li><strong>When stop begins.</strong> The daemon closes intake, refuses commands still waiting for admission, and gives admitted work
        up to 30 seconds to finish.</li>
      <li><strong>At 30 seconds.</strong> It interrupts workers that are still running. Command replies and Mailer cleanup share the rest
        of the shutdown time.</li>
      <li><strong>At 60 seconds.</strong> The standalone daemon exits if a worker or cleanup is still blocked. The clock starts at the first
        accepted stop. Repeating the command or disconnecting the stop client leaves that deadline in place.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="wide">To replace the daemon, run <code>sjm daemon restart</code> instead. It waits for the old process to exit before
      starting a new one. Starting again can also recover discovery files left by a forced exit. Ordinary Java <code>Mailer.close()</code>
      still drains gracefully.</p>
    <p class="wide"><strong>If the send never reported a result.</strong> A successful stop tells you the daemon exited. An interrupted
      invoice may already have been submitted, so check the available result or delivery evidence before deciding whether to resend it.
      The daemon leaves that decision to your job and never automatically resubmits an uncertain command.</p>

    <details class="doc-disclosure wide" id="daemon-request-identity">
      <summary>Status, admission, and repeat requests</summary>
      <p>Status and stop have separate, bounded handling capacity, so slow SMTP work does not take up their workers. When there is no
        room for another command reply, a new command gets a busy response before admission. Status reports reply pressure separately
        from application work. Closing a client connection leaves an admitted command running.</p>
      <p>The daemon remembers completed request identities for the authentication freshness window,
        including allowed future timestamps. If every entry is still protected, it refuses new requests as busy. Repeating an identical
        retained request returns its original result or reports that the result is unavailable, without submitting it again. This
        bounded, in-memory history offers no durable queue or exactly-once delivery guarantee.</p>
    </details>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-sending-limits" id="section-sending-limits" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Sending limits</h3>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">The generated CLI options accept the same counts and ISO-8601 periods as the Java API. Add these to your usual send command,
      or set the equivalent <code>simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.*</code> properties:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">--mailer:withMessageRateLimit 30 PT1M
--mailer:withRecipientRateLimit 100 PT1M
--mailer:withRateLimitGroup company-account
--mailer:withRateLimitBurstsAllowed false</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">These are examples, not implicit limits. Use <code>--mailer:withMessageRateLimit--help</code> for generated help;
      reset options include <code>--mailer:resetMessageRateLimit</code>, <code>--mailer:resetRecipientRateLimit</code>,
      <code>--mailer:resetRateLimitGroup</code>, and <code>--mailer:resetRateLimitBurstsAllowed</code>.</p>
    <p class="wide">A one-shot process starts with fresh local history, so repeating one-shot commands does not enforce a rate across commands.
      A running daemon retains its factory for the configuration environment. A cached Mailer profile keeps its private allowance while retained;
      a named group can share allowance across profiles within that factory when their rules agree. Separate environments, daemon instances,
      or restarts do not share history. This is not an account-wide or durable quota.</p>
    <p class="wide">The command waits for the send, including any rate wait. Validation, connection tests, probes, and logging-only sends consume
      nothing. Read <a href="sending-and-execution.html#section-sending-limits">Sending and execution</a> for counting rules, rolling windows,
      optional spreading, and cancellation/shutdown implications.</p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-usage-help" id="section-usage-help" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Usage Help</h3>

  <section>
    <p>You can get usage information displayed on any level:</p>
    <table>
      <colgroup>
        <col span="1" style="width: 15%;">
        <col span="1" style="width: 40%;">
        <col span="1">
      </colgroup>

      <!-- Put <thead>, <tbody>, and <tr>'s here! -->
      <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Global level</td>
        <td><code class="language-text inline">--help</code></td>
        <td>informs about available commands</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Command level</td>
        <td><code class="language-text inline">send --help</code></td>
        <td>informs about options available for the "send" command</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Option level</td>
        <td><code class="language-text inline">send --email:withSubject--help</code></td>
        <td>displays usage help for the "withSubject" option</td>
      </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="wide">If you're troubleshooting a bad SMTP port, you can still run <code>sjm send --help</code>. Help and version output
      work even with invalid mail configuration. Commands that execute still validate it.</p>
    <ul class="wide">
      <li><strong>Request help.</strong> <code>sjm send --email:withSubject--help</code> displays help for that option and ignores later arguments.
        <code>sjm send --help --mailer:async</code> and <code>sjm send --help --daemon=typo</code> also show help. An invalid daemon selector or retired
        option before help keeps its diagnostic. The same ordering applies in argument files.</li>
      <li><strong>Use option-looking text in a message.</strong> <code>--email:withSubject "How to use --help"</code> sets the subject and the
        command continues. A value such as <code>--mailer:async=false</code> stays literal when a message option consumes it.</li>
      <li id="daemon-selector-values"><strong>Choose the daemon mode first.</strong> Put routing selectors before builder options if a preceding
        option could consume them as values. For example, <code>--email:withSubject "--daemon=off disables persistence"</code> sets that subject.
        This applies to body and header text too, with no extra escaping in either execution mode.</li>
    </ul>
    <img data-src="../../assets/cli_example.png" alt="Example of Simple Java Mail CLI usage" style="width: 100%; margin-top: 1em;">
  </section>


  <a href="#section-at-files" id="section-at-files" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>@-files</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">If your reporting command gets unwieldy, put its arguments in a UTF-8 file named <code>import-report.args</code>
      beside the job. Include the subcommand and any daemon selector. The format follows
      <a href="https://picocli.info/#AtFiles">Picocli's argument files</a>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">send
--email:startingBlank
--email:from jobs@example.org
--email:to ops@example.org
--email:withSubject "Nightly import"
--email:withPlainText "The import finished."</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text">sjm @import-report.args</code></pre>
    <ul class="wide">
      <li><strong>Keep values together.</strong> Use single or double quotes for spaces. Unquoted <code>#</code> starts a comment after the preceding
        value, so <code>"Hello"# note</code> keeps <code>Hello</code> and <code>""# note</code> keeps an empty argument. LF, CR and CRLF all work.</li>
      <li><strong>Reference another file.</strong> Nested files are subject to depth and size limits. Relative names resolve from the client's working
        directory. Expansion runs once before routing, so a missing or unreadable file stays literal even if the daemon has a file with the same name.</li>
      <li><strong>Pass a literal <code>@</code>.</strong> Write <code>@@example</code> to pass <code>@example</code>. Set
        <code>picocli.useSimplifiedAtFiles=true</code> if every non-comment line should be one argument.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-config-properties" id="section-config-properties" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Configuring properties</h3>
  <section>
    <p>To check the relay without sending a message, put this file in <code>etc/simplejavamail.properties</code> in the CLI installation.
      The provided scripts include <code>etc</code> on the classpath. Substitute the relay and port you want to test.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.smtp.host=smtp.example.org
simplejavamail.smtp.port=587
simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-shell">sjm connect</code></pre>

    <p>Supply credentials through your usual configuration source if the relay requires them. The command captures one snapshot for its
      Email and Mailer builders. Environment variables take precedence over file entries, and JVM system properties take precedence
      over environment variables. If your job uses a daemon, restart it after changing its startup configuration.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Failover, command options and locked settings</summary>
<p class="wide">For <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-named">connection failover</a>, define named Mailers in the loaded configuration
      and select one with <code>--mailer:withFailoverMailerName secondary</code>. Generated policy options include
      <code>--mailer:withFailoverPrimaryRecheckInterval PT30S</code>. The daemon keeps different routes, policies, locks and referenced configuration
      material in separate profiles. No additional command or asynchronous send mode is required.</p>
    <p class="wide">An explicit connection option must agree with other explicit loaded declarations by default.
      After reviewing which setup should apply, use <code>--mailer:withConnectionConflictPolicy ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code> for deliberate replacement.
      Locks and mandatory TLS requirements still apply. See <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">connection authority and restrictions</a>.
      Other Email and Mailer settings keep their own precedence or merge rules.</p>
    <p class="wide">
      <a href="configuration.html#section-locked-configuration">Locked configuration</a> uses the same startup sources, for example
      <code>simplejavamail.locked.smtp.host=relay.company.com</code>. There is no separate lock flag: a conflicting builder option such as
      <code>--mailer:withSMTPServer another-relay.example.org 587</code> fails before connecting. The local daemon retains the locked snapshot;
      restart it when changing centrally supplied settings. A command cannot remove an archive recipient required by that snapshot.
    </p>
</details>

  </section>

  <a href="#section-config-logging" id="section-config-logging" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Configuring logging</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Log4j2 configuration is supported and you can edit the provided <code class="inline language-text">log4j2.xml</code> in the
      <code class="inline language-text">etc</code> folder. The daemon uses a separate bounded rolling log that omits raw command values, messages, and credentials.
    </p>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-invoking-cli-manually" id="section-invoking-cli-manually" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Invoking CLI manually</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      If you wish to directly invoke the CLI from the jar or class files, CLI support for Java libraries works
      like any other Java static void main invocation on Java 17 or newer:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">java -cp all;the;jars.jar org.simplejavamail.cli.SimpleJavaMail --help</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Simple Java Mail's own main jar should be on the classpath of course, as well as all non-optional dependencies,
      the optional cli-module dependency and <em>its</em> dependencies.
    </p>
    Add <code class="inline language-text">simplejavamail.properties</code> to this classpath to use the same conventional file, environment, and system-property
    resolution as the provided scripts.
  </section>


  <a href="#section-or-by-maven" id="section-or-by-maven" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Or by Maven</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Alternatively, you can also let Maven deal with the dependencies, by invoking the maven-exec plugin.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">mvn exec:java -Dexec.mainClass="org.simplejavamail.cli.SimpleJavaMail" -Dexec.args="--help"</code></pre>
    Or by configuring it in your POM:
    <pre><code class="small language-xml">
&lt;build&gt;
  &lt;plugins&gt;
    &lt;plugin&gt;
      &lt;groupId&gt;org.codehaus.mojo&lt;/groupId&gt;
      &lt;artifactId&gt;exec-maven-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;
      &lt;version&gt;3.6.3&lt;/version&gt;
      &lt;executions&gt;
        &lt;execution&gt;
          &lt;goals&gt;&lt;goal&gt;java&lt;/goal&gt;&lt;/goals&gt;
        &lt;/execution&gt;
      &lt;/executions&gt;
      &lt;configuration&gt;
        &lt;mainClass&gt;org.simplejavamail.cli.SimpleJavaMail&lt;/mainClass&gt;
        &lt;arguments&gt;
          &lt;argument&gt;--help&lt;/argument&gt;
        &lt;/arguments&gt;
      &lt;/configuration&gt;
    &lt;/plugin&gt;
  &lt;/plugins&gt;
&lt;/build&gt;
    </code></pre>
    <pre><code class="language-text">mvn exec:java</code></pre>
  </section>

  <h2 id="building-cli-from-source">Building the CLI from source</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Run the normal Maven CLI build on JDK 17 or newer. It prepares the command and help data before tests,
      without a separate generation script or editor setup:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">mvn -pl modules/cli-module -am verify</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      The first build saves a reusable pair under the CLI module's Git-ignored <code>.build-cache/metadata</code> directory.
      Later builds reuse it when the inputs match, including after <code>mvn clean</code>. API, Javadoc or generator changes refresh it automatically.
      Generated binaries no longer belong in source control. Release builds generate fresh data and check the finished JAR, ZIP and TAR in new JVMs.
      See the <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/docs/DEVELOPMENT.md#generated-cli-metadata">development guide</a>
      for the cache and package checks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section><p class="wide">Optional <a href="/mail-send-retries.html#section-configuration">retry options</a> are generated from typed builder input:
    <code>--mailer:withMailSendRetryAttempts 3 --mailer:withMailSendRetryDelay PT30S</code>, with optional backoff, jitter and resource limits.
    Sends wait for the final result. Loaded policies, named policies, locks and explicit options all separate daemon profiles when their behavior differs.</p></section>

  <section><p class="wide"><code>--mailer:withMailSendTelemetrySmtpEndpointAttributes true</code> configures the optional
    <a href="/opentelemetry.html">telemetry endpoint permission</a>. It does not supply an SDK or activate telemetry.
    Enable reporting with <code>--mailer:withMailSendTelemetryEnabled true</code> or <code>simplejavamail.telemetry.enabled=true</code>;
    the included optional module uses the application's or Java agent's global instance. No SDK or exporter setup is added to the CLI.
    Activation, permission, locks and explicit options participate in daemon profile identity.</p></section>
